></span></p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">To keep expenses manageable, some owners opt =
to go DIY.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%"><strong>Stacy Pagel</strong> watched instruct=
ional YouTube videos to learn how to groom her Saint Bernard =E2=80=94 GCHG=
 CH Kris's Kountry Three Card Monte V Northstar, or =E2=80=9CPanda=E2=80=9D=
 for short. The 170-pound pup requires one to two double-shampoo and stylin=
g sessions every month, which would run Pagel upwards of $150 per appointme=
nt where she lives in southwestern Wisconsin.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">But Pagel=E2=80=99s approach doesn=E2=80=99t =
exactly come cheap, either. =E2=80=9CYou have to buy the equipment. Dryers =
will cost $100 or $200, and then you have all the different types of scisso=
rs=E2=80=9D =E2=80=94 easily hundreds of dollars altogether =E2=80=94 =E2=
=80=9Cplus the whole process takes around four hours every time,=E2=80=9D s=
he says.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">Custom shampoo and coat conditioner require f=
requent restocks, as do the special pants Panda wears off duty to keep his =
legs clean and white in between baths. (=E2=80=9CIf you let his coat get to=
o wet too often, it=E2=80=99ll turn yeasty.=E2=80=9D)</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">Like a majority of show dog owners, Pagel doe=
s her own handling at competitions instead of paying a professional. But si=
mply entering the ring with leash in-hand is a costly endeavor, requiring t=
he use of <a href=3D"https://d2q-Vf04.na1.hubspotlinks.com/Ctc/UB+113/d2q-V=
f04/VWSg6n2lt59WW8lTDvM4C1FzMVLsY3L5KgzHXN8XrLFz2_3wqW6N1vHY6lZ3q2W77h_8G50=
t128VdSRGv87LL2vW6T-Vg_2gbGPxW19jDpN8pZtrMVybCng4M20mCW3bnWD1846wWPW7KPVV16=
c_VQhW8Zp2pT28HRClW6cy-Dx24Kd6DW1BZyBf3rlfwDW7zWmnW2K7Jr7N69zxxMTKCQWW1k7Ss=
f32WX1PVvFZVq6FM-_HW8xSd9w8S2LvFV4BtrH923nf1W9hDvlb2nthQnW7_mls_5K9j0bVLlXq=
X44fzyqW7kFSFX702VFcW4_Gl8X1Jwd9rW3LKdPw1NBdXsf2gqqw604" style=3D"color:#19=
2733" data-hs-link-id=3D"0" data-hs-link-id-v2=3D"BoVLgGnq" target=3D"_blan=
k">special dog leads</a> and snappy attire. At this year=E2=80=99s event, a=
n onsite vendor stall showcased racks of tweedy skirtsuits, the unofficial =
uniform of Westminster handlers.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">They were priced upwards of $1.5k a pop.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%"><strong>What=E2=80=99s in it for the winners<=
/strong></p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">Though there=E2=80=99s plenty of money to be =
made from the dog show ecosystem, the people behind the dogs aren=E2=80=99t=
 necessarily seeing major windfalls. Most dog shows hand out ribbons and tr=
ophies instead of cash prizes to even their biggest winners; last week=E2=
=80=99s Westminster=E2=80=99s Best in Show champ, Penny the Doberman Pinsch=
er, went home with the Westminster Legend Trophy, a handsome crystal bowl.<=
/p></div></div></td></tr></tbody></table>
